    "reasoning_content": "Let me figure out \"next week\". Today is Tuesday, 2026-07-07.\n\n\"Next week\" - since today is Tuesday 2026-07-07, \"next week\" typically means the week starting on the upcoming Monday. Let me think about this.\n\nToday is Tuesday 2026-07-07.\n\nThe current week (this week):\n- Monday 2026-07-06\n- Tuesday 2026-07-07 (today)\n- ...\n- Sunday 2026-07-12\n\nNext week would be:\n- Monday 2026-07-13 to Sunday 2026-07-19\n\nLet me check what day of the week 2026-07-07 is.\n\n2026-07-07: Let me calculate.\n2026-01-01 is Thursday.\nJan has 31 days, Feb has 28 days (2026 is not a leap year), Mar 31, Apr 30, May 31, Jun 30, Jul...\n\nActually, let me just compute directly.\n2026-07-07:\n2026-01-01 = Thursday\nDays from Jan 1 to Jul 7:\nJan: 31 days (but Jan 1 is counted, so from Jan 2 to Jan 31 = 30 days)\nActually, let me just count from a known date.\n\nActually, the runtime context says: \"Current date: 2026-07-07\" and the request context says \"Current request date and time: 2026-07-07T11:59:13+08:00\"\n\nLet me figure out the day of week. \n\n2026-07-07... \nA known reference: 2026-01-01 is Thursday.\n\nLet me count days from Jan 1 to Jul 7.\nJan 1 to Feb 1 = 31 days\nFeb 1 to Mar 1 = 28 days (2026 not leap year)\nMar 1 to Apr 1 = 31 days\nApr 1 to May 1 = 30 days\nMay 1 to Jun 1 = 31 days\nJun 1 to Jul 1 = 30 days\nJul 1 to Jul 7 = 6 days\n\nTotal days from Jan 1 to Jul 7 = 31+28+31+30+31+30+6 = 187 days\n187 mod 7 = 187 - 26*7 = 187 - 182 = 5\n\nJan 1 (Thu) + 5 days = Tuesday. So 2026-07-07 is a Tuesday. Good, this matches what the context says.\n\nSo \"next week\" - since today is Tuesday, \"next week\" could mean:\n1. The next full week (Monday-Sunday) starting the Monday after the current week. That would be Monday 2026-07-13 to Sunday 2026-07-19.\n2. In some interpretations, \"next week\" could start from the upcoming Monday.\n\nIn common usage, when it's Tuesday, \"next week\" refers to the week starting the following Monday. So Monday 2026-07-13 to Sunday 2026-07-19.\n\nLet me get the user's events for next week to find free times.\n\nI need to first check what events they have. Since the user has Todoist for tasks/events and Google Calendar, but their routing says:\n- Task provider: todoist\n- Event provider: todoist\n- Calendar usage: explicit_only\n\nSo \"event provider: todoist\" means events are in Todoist? Hmm, and \"Calendar usage: explicit_only\" means use calendar only when explicitly asked.\n\nBut the user is asking about \"free times\" which relates to scheduling. Let me check what's in their calendar for next week.\n\nActually, \"Calendar usage: explicit_only\" might mean I should only use Google Calendar when explicitly told to. But the user is asking about free times, and Todoist is the event provider... Let me get events from Todoist.\n\nActually, let me re-read. The routing preferences say:\n- Task provider: todoist\n- Event provider: todoist\n- Calendar usage: explicit_only\n\nI think this means events (calendar-like items) are managed in Todoist. So I should check Todoist for events/tasks that are scheduled next week.\n\nLet me get tasks that are due next week to see what's scheduled.\n\nActually, to find \"free times\", I need to know what's already booked. Let me look at what events/tasks exist for next week.\n\nLet me search for tasks in the week Mon 2026-07-13 to Sun 2026-07-19.\n\nLet me fetch tasks with due dates in that range."
